    ],
    "justification": "Two observable conditional residuals now distinguish pairwise-score optimality from equality with the full information bound.",
    "gap": "The aggregate KKT equation alone cannot certify full semiparametric efficiency when period-specific normals are omitted.",
    "consumer": "Software can report both diagnostics rather than labeling every conditional-loading optimum efficient.",
    "proof_tex": "Fix either declared constant comparator and write \\(b=b^{\\mathrm{cmp}}\\).  Both \\(b\\) and \\(b^\\star(X)\\) satisfy \\(A'b=\\omega\\).  Every coordinate of \\(\\Psi\\) in target block \\(k\\) has conditional mean \\(h_k(X)\\), so both aggregate scores have conditional mean \\(\\omega'h(X)\\).  Put \\(d=b-b^\\star=Nz\\).  Lemma \\(\\ref{lem:singular-operator}\\) gives \\(N'\\Gamma b^\\star=0\\), and hence, pointwise,\n\\[\nb'\\Gamma b-b^{\\star\\prime}\\Gamma b^\\star\n=2d'\\Gamma b^\\star+d'\\Gamma d=d'\\Gamma d=z'Hz.\n\\tag{23}\n\\]\nThe conditional variance formula and integration prove the first displayed identity in the proposition.  Adding the identity \\(V_{\\mathrm{agg}}-V_{\\mathrm{can}}=\\Delta_{\\mathrm{normal}}\\) from Theorem \\(\\ref{thm:complete-normal-canonical-gradient}\\) proves the full-loss formula.\n\nMoreover,\n\\[\nN'\\Gamma b=N'\\Gamma(b-b^\\star)=Hz.\n\\tag{24}\n\\]\nIf (24) is zero, then (23) is zero.  Conversely, if the expected quantity in (23) is zero, nonnegativity gives \\(z'H(X)z=0\\) almost surely.  Positive semidefiniteness then gives \\(H(X)z=0\\), so (24) vanishes.  This proves the aggregate equality characterization, including singular cases.\n\nIt remains to characterize full equality.  The difference \\(b'\\Psi-\\psi_P^0\\) is a measurable clean-comparison contrast and hence belongs to \\(\\mathcal C_P\\subseteq\\mathcal T_P^\\perp\\) by Theorem \\(\\ref{thm:observable-equivalence}\\).  Therefore \\(b'\\Psi\\) is another ambient gradient of the same target, and its projection onto \\(\\mathcal T_P\\) is the unique canonical gradient.  The complete-normal tangent theorem says that\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_P^\\perp\n=\\overline{\\{a(X)'Z_P^{\\mathrm{per}}:a\\text{ measurable}\\}}.\n\\]\nThus \\(b'\\Psi\\in\\mathcal T_P\\) if and only if it is orthogonal to every such multiple.  Conditional expectation shows that this is equivalent to\n\\[\nE_P[Z_P^{\\mathrm{per}}b'\\Psi\\mid X]=0\n\\quad P_X\\text{-almost surely}.\n\\tag{25}\n\\]\nAn ambient gradient has variance equal to the canonical bound if and only if its orthogonal normal component is zero, so (25) is also equivalent to full efficiency.  By Definition \\(\\ref{def:projection-diagnostic}\\), (24) and (25) are exactly the two reported population residuals.",
